     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "title": "How it works",
+      "content": "Free indirect speech is like reported speech in two ways: it keeps the third person and the past tenses of the narration (*estaba*, *llegaba*, and the conditional for what is still to come). It is like direct speech in two others: it keeps the character's questions and exclamations, and the character's own point of view. That is why a word like *mañana* can stay next to a past verb: *Luis abrió la carta. ¡Por fin! Mañana mismo se lo diría a todos.*"
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "title": "Four clues",
+      "content": "• There is no *pensó que*, *dijo que* or *se preguntó*, and no quotation marks.\n• The sentence is a question, a doubt or an exclamation inside the narration.\n• The time and place words belong to the character: *ahora*, *mañana*, *aquí*, *aquello*.\n• The verb is in a past tense, but the thought sounds like the character speaking."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
+      "items": [
+        {
+          "spanish": "Ana miró el reloj. ¿Dónde estaba Pedro?",
+          "english": "Ana looked at the clock. Where was Pedro?"
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "Luis abrió la carta. ¡Por fin! Mañana mismo se lo diría a todos.",
+          "english": "Luis opened the letter. At last! He would tell everyone tomorrow."
+        },
+        {
+          "spanish": "El jefe leyó el informe. ¿Quién lo había escrito? Aquello no tenía sentido.",
+          "english": "The boss read the report. Who had written it? That made no sense."
+        }
+      ]
+    },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "Para reconocer el discurso indirecto libre en un texto literario B2, busca oraciones interrogativas o exclamativas en tercera persona del pasado que expresen la perplejidad íntima de un personaje sin que aparezca la fórmula explícita *'él se preguntaba'*. Es la marca suprema de la introspección narrativa moderna."
+      "content": "You only need to recognise this form, not to write it. When a narrated sentence suddenly asks a question or exclaims, ask: whose thought is this? If it is a character's, it is free indirect speech."
     }
   ]
 }
