+          "Según el vecino, la tienda cerró."
         ],
         [
-          "insinuar que",
-          "The columnist hinted that private interests guided the legislative vote."
+          "al parecer",
+          "Al parecer, el director ya había salido."
         ],
         [
-          "conjeturar que",
-          "Analysts conjectured that the border dispute would be resolved diplomatically."
+          "supuestamente",
+          "Supuestamente, el contrato ya estaba firmado."
         ],
         [
-          "presumir que",
-          "Investigators presumed that the documents were stored in the vault."
+          "presunto + noun",
+          "el presunto culpable (the alleged culprit)"
         ],
         [
-          "especular con que",
-          "The markets speculated that the interest rates would rise next quarter."
+          "dar a entender que + indicative",
+          "Dio a entender que sabía más."
         ],
         [
-          "dar a entender que",
-          "The spokesperson implied that significant policy changes were underway."
+          "se dice que + indicative",
+          "Se dice que la tienda cerró el mes pasado."
         ]
       ]
     },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "title": "Saying it, or passing it on",
+      "content": "Compare *El río había subido mucho* (you say it is so) with *Según una vecina, el río había subido mucho* or *Al parecer, el río había subido mucho* (you pass it on). These tools are followed by the indicative, because you are reporting what is said or seen, not rejecting it. *Supuestamente* and *presunto* go further: they suggest that the claim may not be true."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "text",
+      "title": "A form to recognise",
+      "content": "In newspapers you will meet a conditional used to pass on a rumour: *El ministro habría dicho que no vendría* (\"The minister reportedly said that he would not come\"). The conditional tells you that the writer reports it without confirming it. Some Spanish style guides advise against it in careful writing, but you will see it in the news, so learn to recognise it. You do not need to use it."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
